.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH snip-config 1  "snip 0.5.0" 
.SH NAME
snip\-config \- Inspect and modify ~/.config/snip/config.toml
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsnip config\fR [\fB\-\-library\fR] [\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Inspect and modify ~/.config/snip/config.toml
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-library\fR \fI<LIBRARY>\fR
Library root. Falls back to SNIP_LIBRARY, ancestor discovery, then config
.TP
\fB\-\-output\fR \fI<OUTPUT>\fR
Structured output mode for commands that return records
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
human
.IP \(bu 2
json
.IP \(bu 2
jsonl
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<COLOR>\fR
Color policy for terminal preview
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.IP \(bu 2
always
.IP \(bu 2
never
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
snip\-config\-path(1)
Print the resolved config file path
.TP
snip\-config\-show(1)
Print the current config (defaults to an empty schema when absent)
.TP
snip\-config\-init(1)
Create a config file, optionally binding a default library
.TP
snip\-config\-set(1)
Set one supported config key
.TP
snip\-config\-unset(1)
Remove one supported config key and restore its built\-in default
